    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/493313345/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """Monday, Feb. 4, 1907
Ice on pond. Clear, very windy, cold.
I felt ill, so stayed in, and was in fact in bed when Lady Markby called.
Bernhard sat at his desk grappling with — nothing — he says, for his ideas refuse to come.
We were speaking of Rashdall, whom I said Geoffrey Scott rather despised. “But who knows? Young people are so arrogant!” Bernhard said they were like undrained tracts of land, perhaps full of water, but you couldn’t tell till they had been canalized how much god water there really was. And before any channel has been made, they despise the little oozings and rills that come from other people’s marshes!"""^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 57 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1907> .

